---
import { runFor, shownSeconds } from "../../../lib/benchmarks";
import Speed from "../speed.astro";

// The ESLint toolchain against both native engines. The single-file time is
// the median of six runs of `ultracite check components/button.tsx` (the
// component on this page) in a project like the benchmark's, on the same
// machine and versions.
const SINGLE_FILE_SECONDS = 2.8;
const small = runFor(42);
const large = runFor(420);
---

<Speed
  highlight="eslint"
  id="es-speed"
  stats={[
    {
      label: "To check the one file an agent just edited",
      unit: "s",
      value: String(SINGLE_FILE_SECONDS),
    },
    {
      label: "To check 42 files",
      unit: "s",
      value: String(shownSeconds(small.ms.eslint)),
    },
    {
      label: "To check 420 files",
      unit: "s",
      value: String(shownSeconds(large.ms.eslint)),
    },
  ]}
  title="The trade-off is time"
  toolchains={["eslint", "oxlint", "biome"]}
>
  ESLint runs in Node and asks TypeScript’s type checker for the rules that
  read types, then Prettier and Stylelint run after it. That’s seconds where
  the native engines take a third of one. In an agent’s loop it’s less,
  because the hook only fixes the file that changed.
</Speed>
